      "message": "rpc: Add RPC_METHOD_NOT_PERMITTED error for -rpcwhitelist denials\n\nReturn a JSON-RPC error with a new RPC_METHOD_NOT_PERMITTED (-38) code when\n-rpcwhitelist refuses a request, with HTTP status 200 for JSON-RPC 2.0 requests\nand 403 for legacy 1.x requests. Previously every refused request got an empty\nbody and HTTP 403, so the status was the only sign of the refusal.\n\nThis makes a whitelist denial follow the rule every other failed method call\nhas followed since bf1a1f16624 (#27101): the error goes in the response body,\nand a 2.0 request gets HTTP 200 unless the HTTP request itself is invalid. A\nrefused 2.0 notification gets HTTP 204 and no body, like other notifications.\nJSONErrorReply() asserts this rule, and doc/JSON-RPC-interface.md documents it\nfor 2.0 requests, so the 403 that refused 2.0 requests got contradicted the\ndocumentation. This change brings the behavior in line with it, and the\ndocumentation needs no change.\n\nThe empty 403 reply broke bitcoin-cli in two ways. Over -ipcconnect it gets the\nreply through interfaces::Rpc::executeRpc, which carries no HTTP status, so it\nprinted a JSON type error. Over HTTP it sends 2.0 for every request and stops\nat a 403 status without reading the body, so it printed only \"server returned\nHTTP error 403\". Both now print the permission error.\n\nThe denial is reported at the point where a failing method reports its error,\nso the existing code handles the version differences and notifications, and the\nonly new whitelist logic is a WhitelistDenial() check. The same requests are\nrefused and their methods do not run. One difference: a request has to be parsed\nbefore it can be answered in its own version, so a user refused outright by\n-rpcwhitelistdefault is no longer turned away before parsing. A malformed request\nfrom that user now gets the usual parse error, and an empty batch gets an empty\nreply.\n\nFor review:\n\n- HTTP clients that send \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\" and check for a 403 status need to\n  read the error code instead. Legacy 1.x clients see the same status as\n  before, now with an error body.\n- Code name: RPC_METHOD_NOT_PERMITTED could instead be a more general\n  RPC_ACCESS_DENIED. Reusing RPC_INVALID_REQUEST or RPC_METHOD_NOT_FOUND would\n  conflict with JSONErrorReply(), which maps them to 400 and 404 for 1.x\n  requests.\n- A batch is still refused as a whole when any of its methods is denied, so the\n  permitted requests in it do not run. Running them would change which requests\n  execute, not just what is reported. The next commit keeps this and reports\n  the refusal per request.\n\nThis issue was reported privately as part of a security audit.\n\nThis change was written with Claude Opus 5.",
