      "message": "rpc: Add RPC_REQUEST_NOT_EXECUTED error for refused -rpcwhitelist batches\n\nWhen -rpcwhitelist refuses a batch, return HTTP 200 with an array holding one\nerror per request: RPC_METHOD_NOT_PERMITTED for each denied method and a new\nRPC_REQUEST_NOT_EXECUTED (-39) code for the other requests, which did not run.\nPreviously the whole batch got HTTP 403 and a single error object (an empty\nbody before the previous commit). The batch is still refused as a whole before\nany request runs, so a refused batch has no partial effect.\n\nBatches report every other error per request in an HTTP 200 response, whatever\nthe JSON-RPC version, and batch clients cannot read a single error object.\nbitcoin-cli -getinfo sends a batch, so a refused one reported \"Batch must be an\narray\" from JSONRPCProcessBatchReply over -ipcconnect, and \"server returned\nHTTP error 403\" over HTTP. It now prints the permission error. Every denial in\nthe batch is reported, not only the first, so one reply names every method the\ncaller needs to drop.\n\nThe authorization pass also no longer fails the whole batch on a malformed\nrequest. It threw for a request that was not an object, and let an absent or\nnon-string method field throw out of get_str(), which failed the whole batch\nwith HTTP 400 or 500. Without a whitelist those requests are reported\nindividually and the rest of the batch still runs. The pass now leaves them to\nthe loop that executes the batch, so both paths agree. Nothing slips past the\ncheck: parsing such a request fails there before its method would run.\n\nRequests without an id are left out of the reply, because JSON-RPC 2.0 gives\nnotifications no response, so a refused batch of only notifications gets HTTP\n204 and no body, like an executed one.\n\nFor review:\n\n- The status of a refused 1.x batch changes from 403 to 200 too, matching how\n  1.x batches report every other error.\n- RPC_REQUEST_NOT_EXECUTED is documented as batch-specific, but the name would\n  also fit any request refused before it runs.\n\nThis issue was reported privately as part of a security audit.\n\nThis change was written with Claude Opus 5.",
